  "body": "Hello,\r\n\r\nCurrently `WebSocket.send()` stores the message in a buffer and returns immediately.\r\nThere is no guarantee that the message will ever be sent. \r\n\r\nThe only hint is that `bufferedAmount` may increase if the WebSocket closes in the meantime, but it is difficult to find out which message has actually been sent (requires to keep a duplicate of every message, which is terrible for performance).\r\nAlso, `send()` throws an error when the WebSocket is in readyState `CONNECTING`, but fails silently when the WebSocket gets closed, which can easily occur with bad Wifi (high latency and dropped packets).\r\n\r\nNode.js `ws` library offers a callback on the server-side that is called when the message has been successfully sent through the network.\r\n\r\nThe current WebSocket browser specification lacks this kind of feedback. \r\nWhen searching the internet about this problem, the comments commonly found are \"_WebSockets are unreliable_\". It should not be so!\r\n\r\n`WebSocket.send()` should return a `Promise` that resolves when the message has been successfully sent through the network, and rejects when the message won't be able to be sent (and provides the message that failed).\r\n\r\nThank you for helping the Internet become a better medium!\r\n\r\n",
